  "ai_language_tip": "English is official in all four and is what offices, hotels and shops answer in; Tok Pisin, Fijian, Pijin and Bislama are not on Bageo's list; some Vanuatu businesses prefer French (fr).",
  "english_ok": "mostly — Business English is normal in Port Moresby, Suva, Honiara and Port Vila; village stores and markets may answer in Tok Pisin, Pijin, Bislama or Fijian.",
  "politeness": "Relaxed and friendly but respectful; greet first (bula in Fiji), ask after the person, then the request; never rush or sound bossy.",
  "name_tip": "People commonly give a first name only; Fijian, Indo-Fijian and Melanesian names are unfamiliar to many systems, so spell each one as-in.",
  "phone_tip": "PNG mobiles have 8 digits starting 7; Fiji numbers have 7 digits (mobiles often 7, 8 or 9 first); Solomon and Vanuatu mobiles have 7 digits; no trunk zero in any of them.",
  "time_tip": "12-hour in speech with am/pm; 'Fiji time' and island time mean starts drift, so confirm the hour and whether it is firm.",
  "number_tip": "Currencies differ: kina (PNG), Fiji dollar, Solomon dollar, vatu (Vanuatu); say the currency name with every price.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid Sunday (church and family day; many shops shut), lunch hour and after 18:00; Christmas to New Year many small businesses close.",
  "closed_days": "Offices close Saturday and Sunday; many shops close Sunday or open short hours; national days differ by country.",
  "booking_channels": "Facebook pages and Messenger are the main booking line, with WhatsApp in Fiji and Viber in places; mobile money such as M-PAiSA (Fiji) and CellMoni (PNG) is common; taxis are booked by phone.",
  "voice_menu": "Digicel, Vodafone Fiji and banks run English IVR; small businesses answer directly.",
  "callback_habits": "Businesses reply on Messenger, sometimes the next day; guesthouses call back from a personal mobile; resorts confirm by email.",
